This is unnecessarily tedious.  Someone else listed a shell script to fix
the files, but simpler than that even would be to use the -a option with
unzip.  This option converts carriage return/line feeds to what is appropriate
for your system type.  I'm using version 5.0 of UnZip, copyright by
S.H.Smith and InfoZip, on a Unix system.

There still remained a couple problems for me to compile ++ on an
Ultrix system, using its native compiler.  First of all, in the
original archived 3.0 source, one of the mail-related #defines isn't
correct for Ultrix, preventing the mailer demon from being compiled
in.  Lines 79 and 80 in mail.c I changed to:

#  ifdef UNIX
#   define MAILPATH "/usr/spool/mail/"

instead of "#ifdef BSD".

Newly created compilation bugs I found in ++ are an #ifdef without an
#endif somewhere in mklev.c, the functions in mkroom.c in lines 30-39 are
later declared as not static, and MSDOS is always #defined by the logic
near the top of config.h.

